<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Teófilo Braga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1843">1843</a>–<a href="/years/1924">1924</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Teofilo Braga</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Teófilo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Braga</a> (1843–1924) was a Portuguese writer, literary historian and positivist thinker who led the provisional government of the First Portuguese Republic after the revolution of 5 October 1910. He served again briefly as president in 1915, after the resignation of Manuel de Arriaga. His many works include a long history of Portuguese literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teofilo-braga.jpg" alt="Teófilo Braga" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fotografia Vasques · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Te%C3%B3filo_Braga_(1915)_-_Fotografia_Vasques,_Lisboa_(Museu_da_Presid%C3%AAncia_da_Rep%C3%BAblica).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joaquim Teófilo Fernandes Braga was born in São José, Ponta Delgada, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azores">Azores</a>, the youngest of seven children, three of whom died in infancy. His mother died when he was three, and his father, a former artillery lieutenant who taught at the local secondary school, married a woman who treated the boy badly. He took refuge in books and at fifteen, in 1859, published a book of verse, Folhas Verdes. He arrived at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a> in April 1861 and joined its Faculty of Law, living austerely.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At Coimbra Braga took part, on the edge of the group, in the Coimbra Question of about 1865, a quarrel between the older writer António Feliciano de Castilho and young dissenters including Antero de Quental. His reply to Castilho was As Teocracias Literárias, and he later wrote that the controversy left him shunned by critics and publishers and turned him from poetry to scholarship. In 1872, the year he took his law degree, he won a chair in modern literature in a public competition.</p><p>Under the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auguste-comte">Auguste Comte</a> he became a positivist, writing Traços Gerais de Filosofia Positiva and Sistema de Sociologia. His other works include the História da Literatura Portuguesa, studies of popular poetry and folklore, and the four-volume História da Universidade de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a>. Critics accused him of omitting citations, and Sílvio Romero called him the Father of Charlatans. In politics he stood for deputy in 1878 as an independent federalist republican and wrote the História das Ideias Republicanas em Portugal in 1880.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Braga</a> joined the Political Directorate of the Republican Party on 1 January 1910. The revolution began on the morning of 4 October 1910 and lasted until the next day, and he was acclaimed President of the Provisional Government. In May 1915, after Manuel de Arriaga resigned following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of General Pimenta de Castro, the assembly elected Braga to the presidency.</p><p>A widower who lived austerely, he walked about even as president with an umbrella or cane, and he lost his sight late in life. He asked in his will for a civil burial without ceremony and died on 28 January 1924, aged 80. He was buried in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeronimos-monastery">Jerónimos Monastery</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belem">Belém</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Teófilo Braga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…accepted, the composer Alfredo Keil, at the suggestion of a group of friends that included Rafael Bordalo Pinheiro and <b>Teófilo Braga</b>, wrote the melody of &quot;A Portuguesa&quot; as a patriotic protest march, and the writer Henrique Lopes de Mendonça agreed to Keil's request for words to fit it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/a-portuguesa">A Portuguesa</a></li><li><blockquote>On 5 October 1910 a revolt by soldiers, sailors and Carbonária militants overthrew Manuel II, and a provisional government under <b>Teófilo Braga</b> proclaimed the republic, ending the Kingdom of Portugal.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-portuguese-republic">First Portuguese Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ponta Delgada, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.74,-25.67&amp;year=1895" aria-label="Ponta Delgada on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Teófilo Braga"><image href="/maps/globes/g330n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5293 4335h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5293 4335h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.74,-25.67&amp;year=1895">What was here in 1895? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
